## Proof

Let $F$ be a field of characteristic zero. Since $\operatorname{char} F = 0$, the canonical ring homomorphism $\mathbb{Z} \to F$ is injective, so we may regard every integer as an element of $F$ via this natural embedding. Thus $\mathbb{Z} \subseteq F = \mathbb{A}_F^1(F)$ is an infinite subset of the affine line.

Recall that in the Zariski topology on $\mathbb{A}_F^1$, a subset $A \subseteq \mathbb{A}_F^1$ is closed if and only if it is the common zero locus of some collection of polynomials:
$$A = V(S) := \{x \in \mathbb{A}_F^1 : f(x) = 0 \text{ for all } f \in S\}, \qquad S \subseteq F[x].$$

Since $F[x]$ is a principal ideal domain, the ideal $I = (S)$ generated by $S$ is principal, say $I = (g)$ for some $g \in F[x]$. Then
$$A = V(S) = V(I) = V(g).$$

We now distinguish two cases.

**Case 1: $g \neq 0$.** Then $g$ is a nonzero polynomial of finite degree $d = \deg g \geq 0$. A nonzero polynomial in one variable over a field has at most $d$ roots, so $V(g)$ is a finite set with at most $d$ elements. By hypothesis $\mathbb{Z} \subseteq A = V(g)$, which would force the infinite set $\mathbb{Z}$ to be contained in a finite set — a contradiction. Hence this case cannot occur.

**Case 2: $g = 0$.** Then $I = (0)$, which means every polynomial in $S$ is the zero polynomial (equivalently $S \subseteq \{0\}$). The zero polynomial vanishes at every point of $\mathbb{A}_F^1$, so
$$A = V(0) = \mathbb{A}_F^1.$$

**Conclusion.** The only case compatible with the hypothesis $\mathbb{Z} \subseteq A$ is Case 2. Therefore any Zariski-closed subset $A$ of $\mathbb{A}_F^1$ that contains $\mathbb{Z}$ must be the entire affine line:
$$\boxed{A = \mathbb{A}_F^1.}$$

The essential point is that a nonzero univariate polynomial over a field has only finitely many roots, so it cannot vanish on the infinite set $\mathbb{Z}$; the only polynomial vanishing on all of $\mathbb{Z}$ is the zero polynomial, whose zero locus is the whole space.

